.\" Checked against the real grammar by `testTheManPageDocumentsEveryFlag` in
.\" src/cli_test.j: every long flag the parser declares has to appear here, and every
.\" flag named here has to exist in the parser. Add a flag to src/cli.j and this page
.\" fails the suite until it is documented.
.TH JSNAKE 1 "2026-10-01" "jsnake 0.1.0" "Games"
.SH NAME
jsnake \- multiplayer snake game for the Linux console
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B jsnake host
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B jsnake join
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B jsnake solo
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B jsnake list
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B jsnake
.BR \-\-version | \-\-help
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B jsnake
is a multiplayer snake game for the Linux console, written in Jennifer. Players find
each other by UDP broadcast, so nobody has to type an address; the game itself is
played over either TCP or UDP, and empty seats can be filled by computer opponents at
four difficulty levels.
.PP
One host owns the game. It is the only process that advances the world: clients send
the direction they want and draw what they are sent, so there is no disagreement to
resolve and a lost packet costs at most one frame. The host opens on a title screen
where every setting can be changed while players arrive, and the game starts when the
host says so.
.PP
Nobody picks a field size. Every player reports the terminal they are sitting at, and
the host plays on the largest field the smallest terminal can show, separately in each
direction. See
.B THE FIELD
below.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B host
Host a game others can join. Opens on the title screen unless
.B \-\-now
is given.
.TP
.B join
Join a game on this network. With no
.B \-\-host
it looks for games by broadcast and offers what it finds as a numbered menu.
.TP
.B solo
Play the computer with no network at all: no sockets are opened and no broadcasts are
answered. Starts at once unless
.B \-\-setup
is given.
.TP
.B list
Print the games on this network and exit. Draws nothing and needs no terminal, so it
works over
.BR ssh (1)
and in a pipe \- which makes it the quickest way to tell a discovery problem from a
game problem.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Global
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Show help and exit. Accepted for each subcommand too, where it describes that
subcommand's own options.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit. On a release build this is a SemVer tag; anywhere else it
carries the commits since that tag and the commit it was built from.
.SS host
.TP
.BI \-m ", " \-\-mode " MODE"
Transport: \fBtcp\fR or \fBudp\fR. Default \fBudp\fR. UDP caps the field at about
9,300 cells, because a whole state line has to fit one datagram; TCP has no such limit.
.TP
.BI \-p ", " \-\-port " PORT"
Game port to listen on. Default \fB47475\fR. Discovery always uses \fB47474\fR.
.TP
.BI \-n ", " \-\-name " NAME"
Your name, shown to others. Default \fBjsnake\fR.
.TP
.BI \-w ", " \-\-width " WIDTH"
Maximum field width. A cap, not a request: it can make the field smaller than the
terminals would allow, never larger. Default \fB240\fR, the game's own limit, which
does not bind in practice.
.TP
.BI \-\-height " HEIGHT"
Maximum field height, on the same terms. Default \fB100\fR.
.TP
.BI \-\-tick " TICK"
Milliseconds per move; lower is faster. Default \fB125\fR, which is eight moves a
second.
.TP
.BI \-\-players " PLAYERS"
How many players to allow, 1 to 8. Default \fB8\fR. Seats are counted, not kinds: see
.B \-\-bots
below.
.TP
.BI \-b ", " \-\-bots " BOTS"
How many computer players to add. Default \fB0\fR. Bots fill the seats that
.B \-\-players
leaves after the humans, so
.B \-\-players 8 \-\-bots 8
seats one human and seven bots rather than refusing. The title screen shows the number
it settled on.
.TP
.BI \-l ", " \-\-bot\-level " LEVEL"
Computer skill: \fBeasy\fR, \fBnormal\fR, \fBhard\fR, or \fBexpert\fR. Default
\fBnormal\fR. Higher levels look further ahead and flood\-fill the space a move leads
into, so they do not trap themselves.
.TP
.BI \-\-on\-leave " POLICY"
What happens when a player drops out: \fBforfeit\fR (their snake leaves with them) or
\fBtakeover\fR (a computer player of the current skill finishes their game). Default
\fBforfeit\fR.
.TP
.BI \-\-lives " LIVES"
Lives per player before they become a spectator, 0 to 9. \fB0\fR means unlimited and
there are then no spectators. Default \fB3\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-wrap
No walls: step off one edge and arrive at the opposite one, as in Pac\-Man. Only the
other snakes can kill you. The frame is drawn dashed rather than solid in that mode.
.TP
.B \-\-plain
Classic rules: plain food only, no specials.
.TP
.B \-\-now
Skip the title screen and start at once.
.TP
.BI \-\-seed " SEED"
Random seed. \fB0\fR picks one from the clock. A fixed seed makes a game reproducible,
which is what the tests and the screenshots in the README use.
.TP
.B \-\-watch
Referee only: run the game without taking a seat. The host's own terminal still counts
toward the field size.
.TP
.B \-\-private
Do not answer discovery broadcasts. Players then need
.B join \-\-host
and the address.
.SS join
.TP
.BI \-H ", " \-\-host " ADDRESS"
Host address as \fIip:port\fR; omit it to go looking by broadcast. Needed across a
VPN, a bridged container, or a firewall that eats \fB255.255.255.255\fR.
.TP
.BI \-m ", " \-\-mode " MODE"
Transport when
.B \-\-host
is given: \fBtcp\fR or \fBudp\fR. Default \fBudp\fR. Ignored otherwise, because a
discovered game announces its own transport.
.TP
.BI \-n ", " \-\-name " NAME"
Your name, shown to others. Default \fBguest\fR.
.TP
.BI \-t ", " \-\-timeout " MS"
How long to look for games, in milliseconds. Default \fB700\fR.
.SS solo
.TP
.BI \-n ", " \-\-name " NAME"
Your name on the scoreboard. Default \fBjsnake\fR.
.TP
.BI \-b ", " \-\-bots " BOTS"
How many computer players to add. Default \fB3\fR.
.TP
.BI \-l ", " \-\-bot\-level " LEVEL"
Computer skill, as for
.BR host .
Default \fBnormal\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-setup
Show the title screen before starting, instead of starting at once.
.PP
.BR \-w ", " \-\-width ", " \-\-height ", " \-\-tick ", " \-\-lives ", " \-\-wrap " and " \-\-plain
mean what they do for
.BR host .
.SS list
.TP
.BI \-t ", " \-\-timeout " MS"
How long to look for games, in milliseconds. Default \fB700\fR.
.SH KEYS
.TP
.B arrow keys, w a s d
Steer. WASD is offered because some terminals and multiplexers swallow arrow keys.
A snake cannot turn back on itself; that key is ignored rather than fatal.
.TP
.B Escape
Back out of wherever you are \- the only key the game navigates with. A host playing a
round returns to its title screen with everyone still seated and every player id
unchanged; a host already on the title screen leaves, having nothing behind it; the final
standings go back to the title screen. A client has no screen of its own behind the game
and so leaves, as does
.B solo
or a
.B \-\-now
host, neither of which ever showed a title screen. Repeated, it always gets you out, one
screen at a time.
.IP
.B It takes two presses.
A lone Escape is never delivered \- the interpreter's keyboard layer reads a second byte
after it with no timeout \- so the pair is what arrives as one Escape. Every key legend
therefore reads
.B esc esc
rather than
.BR esc ;
see
.B BUGS
below.
.TP
.B Ctrl\-C
Stop the program immediately, from any screen. The terminal's own convention rather than
one of the game's keys, and deliberately still reachable after a swallowed Escape, so a
player who pressed Escape once and saw nothing happen is never stuck.
.TP
.B enter, space
Confirm: start the game from the title screen, or begin another round from the final
standings.
.TP
.B up, down, left, right
On the title screen: up and down choose a setting, left and right change it.
.TP
.B r, F5
Refresh the list of games on the join menu.
.TP
.B 1\-9
Pick a game from the join menu.
.PP
No plain letter is a command: apart from
.BR w " " a " " s " " d
the letters do nothing, so a stray keypress cannot end a game.
.SH FOOD
Plain food is \fB*\fR and adds one cell. With specials on \- the default \- four more
can appear, each with its own rate and its own countdown, and each vanishes if nobody
takes it in time.
.TP
.B C
Candy: three cells at once.
.TP
.B V
Vegetables: three cells off. A snake with fewer than three dies eating it.
.TP
.B G
Ghost: ten seconds of passing through other snakes' bodies. Their heads still kill.
.TP
.B !
Toadstool: sudden death.
.PP
The legend under the field names whatever is in play, so the rules are on screen rather
than in this page.
.SH THE FIELD
Every player reports its terminal size on joining, and the host plays the largest field
the smallest terminal can show \- separately in each direction, so a short wide terminal
decides the height and a tall narrow one the width.
.PP
Scoreboard rows are reserved for a full table rather than for whoever is present, so
the field never changes size because somebody joined. Host with
.B \-\-players 2
and the spare rows come back as playing space.
.PP
The title screen always names whichever limit actually bound \- the terminals, a
.BR \-\-width / \-\-height
cap, or the UDP datagram \- so a field smaller than the display tells you which knob
would change it. A player whose window is too small for the agreed field is flagged
there before the game starts.
.PP
.B /usr/share/jsnake/scripts/terminal.j
prints what the game sees: the size the terminal reports, what each player count costs
in scoreboard rows, and where the UDP limit would bite.
.SH NETWORK
.TP
.B UDP 47474
Discovery. A host answers broadcast queries with one line describing its game;
.B \-\-private
turns that off.
.TP
.B TCP/UDP 47475
The game itself, per
.BR \-\-port .
.PP
The protocol is line\-oriented text, one line per message, so a game can be watched
with
.BR nc (1)
and a capture read without a decoder.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Normal exit, including
.BR \-\-help " and " \-\-version .
.TP
.B 1
A usage error, a refused join, or a network error the game could not continue past. The
reason is printed on the terminal the program was started from, after the screen is
restored.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /usr/share/jsnake/
The installed tree.
.B bin/
and
.B src/
must stay siblings: the launcher imports
.B ../src/cli.j
relative to its own file.
.TP
.B /usr/share/jsnake/deck.toml
The app manifest: version, capabilities, and the interpreter floor.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Host a game on this network and wait on the title screen:
.B jsnake host
.TP
Host a fast walled game for four, two of them computer players:
.B jsnake host \-\-players 4 \-\-bots 2 \-\-tick 80
.TP
Host a Pac\-Man\-style game with no specials and one life each:
.B jsnake host \-\-wrap \-\-plain \-\-lives 1
.TP
Referee a game of bots over TCP without playing:
.B jsnake host \-\-watch \-\-bots 6 \-\-mode tcp
.TP
Join whatever is out there, under your own name:
.B jsnake join \-\-name ada
.TP
Join a host discovery cannot reach:
.B jsnake join \-\-host 192.168.1.5:47475 \-\-mode udp
.TP
See what is on the network, from a pipe:
.B jsnake list
.SH BUGS
.B Escape takes two presses.
A lone Escape is never delivered: the interpreter's keyboard layer reads a second byte
unconditionally after one \- to tell a bare Escape from the start of an arrow\-key
sequence \- and has no timed read, so that read blocks until another key is pressed.
Pressing Escape twice decodes the pair as a single Escape, which is why the key works at
all, and why every legend spells it
.BR "esc esc" .
A single Escape followed by a printable key is merged into Alt+that key, so neither press
has any effect; Ctrl\-C after an Escape does still arrive, which is what keeps the
emergency exit reachable.
.PP
This is an upstream limitation, reported to the Jennifer team with reproductions. It needs
a timed read in the interpreter's
.B term
library; when that lands, a single constant in
.B src/keys.j
changes and every legend in the game follows it.
.PP
Report bugs at
.UR https://github.com/jennifer-language/demo-jsnake/issues
.UE .
